From f57072220f98f1794d5e71358211938882ad3ca6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:19:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Ordered assembly: one thread per value of the matrix

The ordered assembly added the stiffness matrices of the elements by one
thread per row of the matrix, each thread searching the columns of its
entries in its row: on the beam of 111 843 unknowns, this kernel took
7.1 ms per assembly on a GPU, against 0.48 ms for the kernel of the atomic
additions, making the assembly of the tangent operator 2.6 times slower.

The stiffness matrices are now added by one thread per value of the
matrix, which adds the contributions to this value, in a fixed order, from
a list computed once for each layout of the rows
(`batch::EntriesContributions`, `batch::computeEntriesContributions`,
from the positions of the entries computed by a kernel), and again if it
does not match the matrix: the column of each contribution is checked by
the kernel (`batch::addStiffnessMatricesByEntries`). The values are the
same as with the previous kernel. The assembly of the tangent operator
takes 6 ms instead of 11 ms (4.3 ms with the atomic additions), plus about
0.13 s at the first assembly.

Without device, the stiffness matrices are added one element after the
other on the host, which is already a fixed order: the ordered assembly
now uses this path, with the positions of the entries, instead of storing
the stiffness matrices (the assembly was 5.7 times slower on the CPU).
